WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.380
Previously, we saw that a couple of thoughts

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.360
were puzzling Nora. And we mentioned that

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.920
Calculus was the key to answer them.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.360
She wanted to know that when we drop a ball,

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.980
how we can find its speed at an INSTANT…

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.880
And while thinking about this, she had another

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.100
strange thought. She was convinced that

00:00:26.101 --> 00:00:27.222
mathematically

00:00:27.222 --> 00:00:30.600
the ball should NEVER reach the floor. This

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.082
thought has puzzled many people for centuries

00:00:33.082 --> 00:00:36.440
and it is called the Zeno’s Dichotomy paradox.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.260
What are your thoughts about these problems?

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.360
There were 2 more interesting problems we

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.300
had discussed. Let’s say we are standing

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.670
on a cliff. Then what is the best way to throw

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.120
a stone such that it covers maximum distance?

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.300
And the other problem was how we can find

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.680
the area of any shape, especially whose boundary

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.500
is made up of curved lines.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.080
We know that Calculus gives us the answers

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.040
to all these problems! The question is ‘How?’

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.220
First, we will focus our attention on these

00:01:11.224 --> 00:01:14.260
three problems. And for this problem, the

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.447
one for which we need to find the angle at

00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:18.740
which to throw the stone, we will have to

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.670
wait till we gain some additional knowledge of calculus.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.600
Let us move on to a new page and start with

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.779
this interesting thought puzzling Nora. She

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.320
thinks that mathematically, the ball should

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.360
never reach the floor. As the ball falls,

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.800
it has to first cover half of the distance

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.100
between its starting position and the floor.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.040
Then it has to cover half of the remaining

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.280
distance. Then it has to cover the next half,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.720
and then the next half and so on.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.119
So the number of steps the ball has to perform

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.800
never ends. Another way of saying it is that

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.039
there are INFINITE number of steps the ball

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:02.331
has to perform. Beware of the term Infinite.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.860
Always remember that Infinite is not a number.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.160
Infinite just means something which NEVER

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.780
ends, or something that is limitless.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.020
Now to perform each step, the ball takes some

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.060
time. Since the number of steps the ball has

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:21.155
to cover never ends that is there are INFINTE

00:02:21.155 --> 00:02:24.379
number of steps, so Nora thinks the ball should

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:25.520
never reach the floor.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.020
But, we know that as we drop the ball, it

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.560
reaches the floor in a few seconds. So where

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.920
is the mistake in Nora’s logic?

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.800
In order to know the time the ball takes to

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.780
reach the floor, we have to add all the ‘individual

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.540
time periods’ taken to perform the individual steps.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.339
But to find this, we will have to add an infinite

00:02:49.339 --> 00:02:53.129
number of terms. Let’s say we take our calculator

00:02:53.129 --> 00:02:56.720
out and start adding these numbers. Then our

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.760
addition will go on and on, and will never

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.747
finish. And as we go on adding the number,

00:03:03.747 --> 00:03:06.380
the sum will get bigger and bigger.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.900
That is, as the ball continues to cover the

00:03:09.911 --> 00:03:12.620
successive steps; the TOTAL time taken by

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:16.120
the ball will keep on increasing. Another

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:18.930
way to say this is the ball will take an infinite

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:21.260
amount of time to reach the floor.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.980
But we know that this isn’t true as its

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.460
common knowledge that the ball will reach

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.060
the floor in a few seconds. It will be a well-defined

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:34.217
number! That means if we add all the time

00:03:34.217 --> 00:03:37.749
periods taken to perform the individual steps,

00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:40.419
we should get a number as our answer.

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:43.450
Yes, even though we can’t mechanically add

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.437
the numbers, we can show that the sum of these

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.260
infinite numbers is equal to a number .

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.860
To show this, we carry out a process known

00:03:52.867 --> 00:03:56.950
as TAKING THE LIMIT. And this is the central

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:00.140
idea that Calculus uses to solve the problems

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.320
we mentioned.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.095
To understand calculus, we need to understand

00:04:05.095 --> 00:04:07.779
the concept of Limits first, which we will

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.220
cover in the upcoming parts!

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:16.120
To understand this paradox, let’s keep things

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.513
simple. We know that as the ball is falling,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.880
its speed increases. But for simplicity, let’s

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.660
assume the speed of the ball to be constant.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.200
Of course we will get the answer even if we

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.525
take the ball’s speed to be increasing,

00:04:32.525 --> 00:04:34.980
but it will be a little bit complicated.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.780
So, let’s say the ball falls with a CONSTANT

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:43.240
speed of half a meter per second. In reality

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.132
the ball’s speed will be more than this,

00:04:46.132 --> 00:04:49.530
but we want to keep it simple. With this speed,

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.660
we know that the ball will take just ‘two’

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:54.100
seconds to cover the distance of one meter

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.500
and reach the floor.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.380
So let’s see how much time the ball takes

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.700
to cover these individual steps.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.820
The time required to cover the first half

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.740
of a meter will be one second. Then to cover

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.420
the next one fourth of the meter, it will

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.993
take half a second. Then to cover the next

00:05:13.993 --> 00:05:16.800
one eighth of the meter, it will be one fourth

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.540
of a second and so on.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:21.840
So did you observed an interesting thing?

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.400
After some steps, the time taken to complete

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.660
the subsequent steps will be very very small.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:32.700
So as the ball completes the successive steps,

00:05:32.708 --> 00:05:35.680
the time taken by it to perform the next step

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.059
will get closer and closer to ZERO. This is

00:05:39.059 --> 00:05:42.360
an important concept. It is getting CLOSER

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.250
to a definite number! But remember it will

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:50.139
not be zero… Each step requires some time,

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.140
however negligible it may be.

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.319
We know that the ball takes 2 seconds to reach

00:05:55.319 --> 00:05:58.180
the floor. Now we can show that if we add

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.110
all these values of time taken by the ball

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:04.560
to perform each step, the sum will NEVER EXCEED

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.560
two and will actually be equal to two.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.980
Before we prove this, let me give you another example.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.980
Consider this square. Let’s say the length

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.409
of each of its sides is one meter. Then we

00:06:19.409 --> 00:06:22.480
know that its area will be one square meter.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.360
Now let’s divide or split this area as we

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:28.105
did with the motion of the ball.

00:06:28.105 --> 00:06:30.880
First we divide the area into two halves.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.200
Then we take one of the two halves and further

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.280
divide it into two halves. So what we get

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:40.500
is two quarters of the square. Now we take

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.800
one of the quarters and again divide it into

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.980
two halves. And we keep repeating this process.

00:06:46.622 --> 00:06:50.180
What is happening here? We will realise that

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.840
this is also a never ending process.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.440
But even though it is a never ending process,

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:58.933
we know that the area of this square, which

00:06:58.933 --> 00:07:01.300
is one square meter, will be equal to the

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:05.140
sum of the areas of these parts. So we see

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:07.680
that the sum of these INFINITE terms is equal

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.100
to one, which is a definite number. We can’t

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.840
manually add all these terms, but their sum

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.480
turns out to be one.

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:19.880
As we go on dividing this square, the area

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:23.340
of its parts get smaller and smaller. They

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.340
get so small that their area is very very

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:29.963
close to zero, but not zero. But because these

00:07:29.963 --> 00:07:33.419
parts as a whole make up the square, the sum

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.310
of their areas cannot be greater than one.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:39.200
So if we keep on adding the areas of these

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.320
parts, their sum will APPROACH one. The word

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.820
‘approach’ is very important here.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:49.880
Anyway, I am not sure if you realised… but

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:51.940
you almost have an answer for the sum of the

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:55.260
time periods taken by the ball to reach the floor.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:58.417
Look at the expression we had for the time

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.300
taken by the ball to reach the floor.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.860
And look at this expression we have for the

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.040
parts of a square.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:08.720
Now if we add one to this set of numbers,

00:08:08.729 --> 00:08:11.771
it becomes the same as this expression. So

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:15.040
if we add 1 to both sides here, we get the

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:19.060
sum of these values as 2. Let me recap!

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.691
On the right hand side of this equality, we

00:08:22.691 --> 00:08:25.340
have these numbers which are same as the time

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:28.600
taken by the ball to cover each steps. And

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:31.340
we get the addition of these infinite terms

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:34.080
to be two , which is exactly the time taken

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.120
by the ball to reach the floor.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:39.620
So , Nora understood where she went wrong

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.440
with her logic! She assumed that the total

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:45.040
time the ball will take to cover these infinite

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.260
steps will be infinite. Remember what we mean

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:53.540
by infinite here. It means something that never ends.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.198
Let’s look at a quick demonstration so that

00:08:57.198 --> 00:09:00.870
you understand better. This is the expression

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.960
we found for the total time taken by the ball

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.340
to reach the floor right? Now let us try to

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:10.280
find the cumulative time taken by the ball

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:12.360
to reach the floor, for each step!

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.480
For example, after 2 steps, the time taken

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:18.920
by the ball will be ‘1 second plus half

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.840
a second’, and that is equal to one point

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.020
five seconds. Similarly, after 3 steps, the

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.460
total time taken by the ball will be one point

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:29.760
seven five seconds.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:33.800
If we continue this for other steps, we see

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:36.690
that the total time taken will get closer

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.326
and closer to two seconds. As the ball covers

00:09:40.326 --> 00:09:43.720
the successive steps, the time taken by it

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:47.420
to cover each step is very very small. So

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.430
even if we continue adding many time periods,

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.720
the total time will never exceed two seconds.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.960
The total time taken will just get closer

00:09:56.960 --> 00:09:59.020
and closer to two seconds.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.280
This is an example to understand the idea

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:05.288
of the Limit process . We can’t find the

00:10:05.288 --> 00:10:08.810
answer to the sum of infinite terms of numbers.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.947
But if we keep on adding successive steps

00:10:11.947 --> 00:10:15.391
we see that the sum approaches a number. With

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:17.954
this, Nora is not confused anymore!

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.020
We see that if we divide the motion of the

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:23.570
ball into an infinite number of steps, it

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:26.420
seems that it will take infinite time to cover

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:29.575
these steps. But we can show that as we add

00:10:29.575 --> 00:10:32.950
the time periods taken in each step, the total

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:36.066
time taken by the ball gets closer and closer

00:10:36.066 --> 00:10:37.520
to a definite number.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.660
It’s similar for the distance the ball covered

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:44.040
too. We had seen how the distance was split

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:47.404
into infinite parts. It doesn’t mean the

00:10:47.404 --> 00:10:51.440
distance is infinite. The sum of these distances

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:53.533
will APPROACH 1 meter.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.520
And guess what, through this limit process

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:00.160
we can also find the instantaneous speed of

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:04.280
the ball. If you remember this was another question that

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:06.780
Nora had. We will discuss

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:10.040
it in the next part. See you there.

